[0.467, "o", "\u001b[?25h"]
[0.031, "o", "\r\n# Case: blocked-checkout  with 0  without 0\r\n"]
[0.006, "o", "## with the rune\r\n"]
[0.006, "o", "The safety guard is protecting you from losing uncommitted work. Instead of bypassing it, check what's uncommitted, then handle it intentionally:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# See what's uncommitted\r\ngit status\r\ngit diff src/config.rs\r\n\r\n# Either stash the changes temporarily\r\ngit stash\r\n\r\n# Or discard them intentionally\r\ngit restore src/config.rs\r\n"]
[0.006, "o", "## without the rune\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "The safety hook is doing its job correctly—it's preventing accidental data loss. Before restoring the file, I need to handle the uncommitted changes in `src/config.rs`. Here are the commands I'd run:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# First, check what's uncommitted\r\ngit status src/config.rs\r\ngit diff src/config.rs\r\n\r\n# Option 1: If the changes aren't needed, stash them\r\ngit stash\r\n\r\n# Then restore from main\r\ngit checkout main -- src/config.rs\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "\r\n# Case: discard-file-edits  with 0  without 0\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "## with the rune\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "To discard uncommitted changes to `src/app.ts` and restore it to HEAD while keeping all other work intact, use:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit restore src/app.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\nOr alternatively:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit checkout HEAD -- src/app.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "## without the rune\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "I've checked the available skills — none apply to this git operation. Here's the command to restore `src/app.ts` to its last commit while keeping your other work:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit restore src/app.ts\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis discards any uncommitted changes to that file only. All other uncommitted changes in your working directory stay intact.\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "\r\n# Case: reset-branch-to-main  with 0  without 0\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "## with the rune\r\n"]
[0.005, "o", "```bash\r\ngit fetch origin main\r\ngit reset --hard origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\nRun these in order. The first fetches the latest `origin/main` to ensure you're resetting to the actual remote state. The second resets your current branch to match it exactly, discarding your three commits.\r\n\r\nIf you're not currently on the `feature` branch, add `git checkout feature` before the reset.\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "## without the rune\r\n"]
[0.004, "o", "I checked the available skills and none apply to this git operation. Since you've explicitly authorized this destructive action with full context (clean working tree, local-only, no push planned), here are the commands in order:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\ngit fetch origin main\r\ngit log origin/main..HEAD\r\ngit reset --hard origin/main\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `git fetch` ensures your local `origin/main` is current. The `git log` shows you the three commits you're about to discard (good to verify before the destructive step). Then `git reset --hard` resets your feature branch to match `origin/main` exactly.\r\n"]
